characters in the pattern treated as literal text; matching the whole string (including rejecting a trailing newline); case sensitivity; newlines inside a field.\n- **Field names:** dotted, hyphenated and `a[b][c]` names, and the cases where those names could clash with each other. A name used twice must match the same text, and using it twice with different types raises `RepeatedNameError`.\n- **Integers:** `d`, `n`, `b`, `o`, `x`/`X`, covering signs, `0x`/`0b`/`0o` prefixes, `,` and `_` grouping, width, zero-padding, and fill and alignment with `=`, `<`, `>`, `^`.\n- **Floats:** `f`, `F` (returns `Decimal`), `e`, `g`, `%`, plus nan/inf, `.0f` (no decimal point) and padding.\n- **Text:** `w W s S D l`, width (a minimum length), precision (a maximum length), alignment, and fill characters that are special in regex.\n- **Dates and times:** every built-in date type (`ti te th tc tg ta tt ts`), including AM/PM handling around 12 o'clock, microseconds and the different timezone formats. Also `strftime`-style formats such as `{:%Y-%m-%d}`, each of which must return the right kind of object (date, time or datetime).\n- **Field positions:** each test with a typed field before another field checks that the later field still comes out right. Types like `g` or `ti` add hidden internal groups, so a miscount there would shift later values. Match positions (`spans`) are checked too.\n- **The rest of the API:** custom types (`with_pattern`, including the hidden group count, and overriding a built-in type), `search` and `findall` with start/end positions, returning raw matches instead of results, and the `Result`, `Match` and `Parser` objects. Also the format-spec splitter, the timezone class, pickling a compiled parser, and the internal helper functions tested directly.\n\n**Expectations I had to change**\nMy first run had 25 failures. In each case I checked the code and found the library was doing what it's written to do, and my expectation was wrong. I changed those tests to match how the library actually behaves. These behaviours may surprise users:\n- A width on a text field counts the padding as part of the value. `{:5}` on `\"   ab\"` returns `\"   ab\"`, not `\"ab\"`.\n- `{:d}` accepts `\"--1\"` and returns -1.\n- With a custom fill and `=` alignment on a float (e.g. `{:x=8.2f}` on `\"xxxx3.14\"`), the fill characters end up in the value, so it raises `ValueError` instead of returning 3.14.\n- `date_convert` treats group index 0 as \"not given\" for month/day (`if mm and dd`). When a timezone name is all capitals, like `EST`, it's passed through as a plain string, and that raises `TypeError` for time-only values.\n- `FixedTzOffset` can't be pickled.\n- I couldn't test `TooManyFields`: this Python has no 100-group limit for regexes, so the error can't be triggered.\n\nI left these behaviours unasserted rather than writing tests that lock them in.","ttft_ms":2653,"type":"result","duration_ms":238275,"uuid":"4fbd90d8-77b6-46a3-a62c-6a31775ac49e","ttft_stream_ms":1622,"time_to_request_ms":183,"first_content_frame_ms":1623,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}

